"""Generate paper notes, figure inventory and LaTeX examples from saved public results."""
import json,sys
from pathlib import Path
import pandas as pd
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]))
from src.utils import REPO_ROOT

def main():
    root=REPO_ROOT;out=root/'docs';out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    prs=pd.read_csv(root/'results/part2/prs_summary.tsv',sep='\t');pairs=pd.read_csv(root/'results/part2/pairwise_correlations.tsv',sep='\t');pair=pairs.iloc[0]
    ov=pd.read_csv(root/'results/week7/snp_overlap_summary.tsv',sep='\t');cases=pd.read_csv(root/'results/week7/case_studies.tsv',sep='\t');genes=pd.read_csv(root/'results/week7/gene_annotation.tsv',sep='\t')
    prov=json.loads((root/'results/week7/provenance.json').read_text());pcs=pd.read_csv(root/'results/part2/pca/explained_variance.tsv',sep='\t');pcn=pd.read_csv(root/'results/part2/pca/variant_counts.tsv',sep='\t').LD_Pruned_SNPs.sum()
    text='''# Part 2 and Week 7: Methods and Results

Generated by `python scripts/make_paper_support.py` from saved tables. Part 1 was preserved.

## Disease score methods

The 90 original TCSC/LDSC files contained Z-scores rather than valid PRS weights. None was scored.
The unused archives were removed after inventorying at the user's request on November 20, 2026;
the historical inventory and cleanup checksums are retained. Current analyses do not require them.
The primary analysis uses GWAS Catalog GRCh37 **formatted `.f.tsv.gz` files** for
Eyre et al. (2012; rheumatoid arthritis, GCST005569) and Mahajan et al. (2018;
type 2 diabetes, European BMI-unadjusted analysis, GCST007517), and the author-hosted
Pardiñas et al. (2018) CLOZUK+PGC2 schizophrenia meta-analysis. Its archive MD5 matches
the author checksum, A1/A2 and OR meanings are documented by the author, and its
IMPUTE2 identifiers are parsed to rsIDs. GRCh37 coordinates are verified using independently
identified panel rsID anchors (counts are in the SNP audit); mismatched positions are excluded.
The portal does not explicitly label this build, so this is an empirical build verification.
The corresponding EBI
`.h.tsv.gz` metadata describe GRCh38 harmonised files and must not be mistaken for the
build of the formatted files used here. RA weights are log(OR); T2D betas are documented
log-odds meta-analysis effects. Source URLs and file checksums are recorded in the repository.

The RA and T2D targeted Immunochip/exome-array studies have limited, nonrandom coverage. They are
educational disease-associated scores, not validated clinical predictors. The three selected diseases meet
the verified input requirements; the Z-only local files remain unsuitable. The schizophrenia
original was completely downloaded and checksum-verified before analysis. Original study
effects and extracted weights remain local because author redistribution conditions apply.

GWAS variants were matched by rsID with GRCh37 position checks; missing T2D rsIDs were
assigned only from unique panel positions. Nonambiguous reverse complements preserve
beta sign while relabeling the counted biological allele. Ambiguous, incompatible,
duplicated and invalid rows were excluded. Stage-specific counts are saved for each disease.
PLINK2 clumping used EUR reference LD, r²=0.1, 250 kb and a fixed p≤0.001 threshold.
Only threshold-eligible variants enter clumping, which is equivalent for retained index SNPs.
Scores sum log-odds beta × effect-allele dosage; missing calls cause an error, not silent
imputation. All people within each public score use the same variants. SD uses ddof=1.
Private comparisons are saved only under ignored `data/private_part2/`; both target and
reference are rescored on their common compatible, nonmissing SNP set. Their values and
plots do not enter this public report. A percentile is a reference rank, not a probability
of disease or an estimated relative risk.

## Disease score results

'''
    for row in prs.itertuples():
        text+=f'- **{row.Disease}:** {row.Original_SNPs:,} original variants, {row.Harmonized_SNPs:,} harmonized, {row.Scored_SNPs:,} clumped/thresholded and scored; {row.Individuals} reference people. Raw mean {row.Mean:.6f}, SD {row.SD:.6f}, median {row.Median:.6f}, Q1 {row.Q1:.6f}, Q3 {row.Q3:.6f}, IQR {row.IQR:.6f}.\n'
    text+=f'''
On the same {int(pair.Individuals)} people, RA and T2D standardized scores have Pearson
r={pair.Pearson_R:.4f} (nominal p={pair.Pearson_P:.4g}) and Spearman r={pair.Spearman_R:.4f}
(p={pair.Spearman_P:.4g}). Their weak positive linear relationship does not establish a shared
causal mechanism. Four SNPs are shared (see `snp_overlap.tsv` for directional fractions
and Jaccard similarity). LD, selected variant overlap, discovery estimates and structure
can all influence these correlations.

## PCA methods and sensitivity analysis

PLINK2 used all 22 autosomes, MAF≥0.05, missingness≤0.02, HWE p≥10⁻⁶ and LD pruning
with a 200-variant window, 50-variant step and r²=0.2. Exact PCA of the variance-standardized
genetic relationship matrix used {pcn:,} variants and 489 public EUR individuals.
All 488 nontrivial eigenvalues were computed so explained fractions use the full eigenvalue
sum. PC1 explains {100*pcs.Explained_Fraction.iloc[0]:.3f}% and PC2
{100*pcs.Explained_Fraction.iloc[1]:.3f}%. This describes within-EUR structure. Validated
population labels were not supplied, so clusters are unlabeled and no groups were inferred
from identifiers. The computation is unadjusted for pedigree relationships; LD pruning and
these basic filters do not exhaust PCA quality control.

Each standardized PRS was regressed on an intercept, PC1 and PC2 for the same people.
The RA–T2D residual Pearson correlation is {pair.PC1_PC2_Adjusted_R:.4f}; this is an exploratory sensitivity
analysis, not proof that ancestry bias is removed. RA–PC1 correlation is recorded in
`prs_pc_associations.tsv`; nominal p-values are descriptive and are not corrected for
multiple exploratory comparisons. Private ancestry results were not calculated or published.

## Week 7 motivation and methods

[Roadmap Epigenomics (2015)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25693563/) maps cell-context
chromatin states; [ENCODE (2020)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2493-4)
integrates biochemical measurements to identify candidate regulatory elements. Accessible
DNA can permit regulatory protein binding. ATAC-seq uses transposase integration to assay
accessibility, rather than measuring gene regulation or causality directly. B cells are
relevant to B-cell-derived lymphoblastoid cell lines (LCLs), but primary B cells differ from
EBV-transformed LCLs in activation, transformation and experimental context.

The existing ENCODE file ENCFF421XIL from ENCSR603LVR is primary B-cell ATAC-seq,
pseudoreplicated narrowPeak (BED6+4), originally GRCh38. Its uncompressed content MD5
matches official ENCODE metadata. UCSC liftOver using hg38ToHg19 and minMatch=0.95
retained {prov['Mapped_Peaks']:,}/{prov['Input_Peaks']:,} peaks and lost {prov['Lost_Peaks']:,}.
Original signal annotations were preserved; original summit offsets were not interpreted
as lifted summit coordinates. File metadata, chain checksum and mapping counts are saved
in `results/week7/provenance.json`. The file predated this work, so its original download
date is unknown; metadata were retrieved November 17, 2026.

BED intervals are [start,end), zero-based; one-based SNP p is represented by [p−1,p).
The saved 6,597,508 Part 1 SNP–gene associations were reused without refitting regressions.
BH and Bonferroni significance use the original genome-wide adjusted p<0.05. Unique
SNPs are counted separately from SNP–gene pairs, using ID/chromosome/position identity.
GENCODE v12 one-based inclusive gene bodies are converted to BED coordinates; gene-body
and ±500 kb window intersections are counted independently for all autosomal protein-coding
genes. Python interval counts support nested peaks, and boundary tests verify endpoints.

## Week 7 results

'''
    for row in ov.itertuples():
        text+=f'- **{row.Set}:** {row.Overlapping_SNPs:,}/{row.Unique_SNPs:,} unique SNPs overlap ({row.Percent:.3f}%); separately, {row.Overlapping_Pairs:,}/{row.SNP_Gene_Pairs:,} SNP–gene associations overlap.\n'
    text+=f'\nAmong {len(genes):,} annotated autosomal protein-coding genes, {genes.Body_Peaks.gt(0).sum():,} have a gene-body peak and {genes.Cis_Window_Peaks.gt(0).sum():,} have a peak in the ±500 kb window. This denominator includes genes without testable expression.\n\n'
    for row in cases.itertuples():
        text+=f'- **{row.Gene}:** lead {row.Lead_SNP} (p={row.Lead_P:.3g}) '+('overlaps' if row.Lead_Overlaps else 'does not overlap')+f' a peak; {row.BH_SNPs_Overlapping} BH-significant and {row.Bonferroni_SNPs_Overlapping} Bonferroni-significant unique cis-SNPs overlap. Gene body: {row.Body_Peaks} peaks; cis-window: {row.Cis_Window_Peaks} peaks.\n'
    text+='''
The case studies are the four strongest genes ranked by the saved Part 1 minimum p-value,
regardless of peak overlap. RPS26 overlaps accessible chromatin at its lead SNP, but this
does not identify a causal SNP or demonstrate that the peak regulates RPS26. A peak
anywhere within ±500 kb is a proximity annotation. Higher overlap among significant SNPs
is descriptive, without a formal enrichment claim: LD, allele frequency, local variant density
and distance from genes violate naive independence assumptions. No optional enrichment
test was performed. The regional figure is a Matplotlib plot, not LocusZoom software output.
'''
    text+='\n## Other disease-pair results\n\n'
    for r in pairs.iloc[1:].itertuples():
        text+=f'- {r.Disease_A} versus {r.Disease_B}: n={r.Individuals}, {r.SNPs_A} and {r.SNPs_B} scoring SNPs; Pearson r={r.Pearson_R:.4f} (p={r.Pearson_P:.4g}), Spearman r={r.Spearman_R:.4f} (p={r.Spearman_P:.4g}); PC-adjusted Pearson r={r.PC1_PC2_Adjusted_R:.4f}.\n'
    (out/'quarter1_part2_week7_methods_results.md').write_text(text)
    inventory=[]
    captions={
     'A_snp_overlap':('Chromatin overlap','Week 7 results','Percentage of unique tested cis-eQTL SNPs overlapping lifted B-cell ATAC-seq peaks, grouped by the original genome-wide significance definitions.','Percentages are descriptive; LD and selection prevent a naive causal or enrichment interpretation.'),
     'B_RPS26_accessibility':('Representative locus','Week 7 results','RPS26 cis-eQTL associations with a lead-SNP marker, B-cell ATAC-seq peak track, and GENCODE v12 gene-body track on GRCh37.','Matplotlib regional plot; accessibility in primary B cells differs from LCLs; overlap is not causality.'),
     'C_gene_overlap':('Gene annotation','Week 7 results','Autosomal GENCODE v12 protein-coding genes overlapping peaks within gene bodies or gene-body plus/minus 500 kb windows.','All annotated genes are the denominator; a nearby peak is not evidence of target-gene regulation.'),
     'prs_distributions':('Score distributions','Part 2 results','Disease-specific log-odds weighted polygenic score distributions among 489 public EUR reference individuals at fixed p≤0.001, clumping r²=0.1 and 250 kb.','Limited targeted-array coverage; raw scores across diseases are not clinical risks or directly comparable.'),
     'prs_correlations':('Cross-disease association','Part 2 results','Pearson correlations of reference-standardized disease scores in the same 489 EUR individuals.','Exploratory association does not establish causal shared biology.'),
     'standardized_prs':('Comparable score scales','Part 2 results','Disease score distributions standardized by the reference mean and sample SD.','Each disease has a different SNP set; z-scores are relative positions, not disease probabilities.'),
     'reference_PCA':('Reference structure','Part 2 methods/results','PC1 and PC2 from LD-pruned autosomal EUR reference genotypes, with explained fractions from all nontrivial eigenvalues.','Within-reference population structure; clusters have no inferred population labels.'),
     'prs_pc_adjusted_correlations':('PC sensitivity','Part 2 supplementary','Pearson correlations after residualizing each standardized disease score on an intercept, PC1 and PC2 in the same people.','Exploratory adjustment does not eliminate ancestry bias.'),
     'snp_overlap':('Shared selected variants','Part 2 supplementary','Jaccard similarities between the selected disease scoring SNP sets.','Overlap alone does not demonstrate pleiotropy.'),
     'pairwise_scatterplots':('Pairwise association','Part 2 supplementary','Pairwise standardized disease scores among the same public reference individuals, with Pearson correlation.','Observational score relationship; LD and discovery estimates may contribute.'),
    }
    captions.update({
      'single_gene_prediction':('Protein-coding expression prediction','Expression prediction results','Observed versus predicted normalized expression in 35 held-out individuals for the strongest training-only protein-coding candidate, alongside validation threshold selection.','Predictive R2 differs from squared Pearson correlation; expression was supplied pre-normalized and no test outcomes selected the model.'),
      'predictive_r2_distribution':('Held-out prediction performance','Expression prediction results','Distribution and evidence-stratified cumulative distributions of predictive R2 in the frozen 150-gene pilot, retaining negative values.','The cohort is stratified by training evidence (44 strong, 52 moderate, 54 weak); it is not a random genome-wide performance estimate. Test size is 35 per gene; one gene (EPCAM, R2=-13.0) is outside the plotted histogram range of most genes and is retained.'),
      'association_strength_vs_prediction':('Training signal and prediction','Expression prediction results','Training-only cis-eQTL association strength versus held-out predictive R2 across the predefined pilot.','Gene selection and SNP counts can influence minimum p-values; the association is exploratory, not causal.'),
      'snp_counts_vs_prediction':('Variant counts and performance','Expression prediction supplementary','Eligible cis-SNP, training-LD clump and selected-model SNP counts versus held-out predictive R2.','Selected counts depend on validation threshold choice; the plots do not measure the causal effect of adding variants.'),
      'gene_characteristics_vs_prediction':('Gene features and performance','Expression prediction supplementary','Gene length, local SNP density, training expression variability, within-gene Bonferroni-significant training SNP count and chromosome versus held-out predictive R2, with Spearman correlations.','Exploratory comparisons in a stratified small cohort with noisy test performance estimates; all 22 autosomes are represented with 4-11 genes each, too few for per-chromosome inference.'),
      'atac_vs_prediction':('Accessibility and prediction','Expression prediction supplementary','B-cell ATAC-seq SNP fractions and cis-window peak counts versus held-out expression predictive R2 on GRCh37.','LD, gene length, density and association strength can confound overlap; primary B cells differ from LCLs and overlap is not a regulatory mechanism.'),
      'threshold_rank_stability':('PRS threshold sensitivity','Part 2 supplementary','Spearman correlations of reference PRS rankings across five predefined P+T thresholds in the same EUR individuals.','No threshold was selected using target outcomes or percentiles; rank agreement does not measure clinical prediction accuracy.'),
      'disease_eqtl_overlap':('Disease variant and eQTL overlap','Part 2 exploratory results','Percentage of tested disease-scoring SNPs with a BH-significant cis-eQTL, compared with harmonized and tested GWAS-background SNPs.','The selected score SNPs are LD-clumped and the background is not; this is descriptive overlap, not formal enrichment or colocalization.'),
      '01_genes_kept_at_each_filtering_step':('Gene eligibility','Part 1 methods','Protein-coding gene counts after annotation, expression and genotype-overlap filtering for the genome-wide cis-eQTL scan.','Counts depend on GENCODE v12, the supplied expression preprocessing and the public EUR panel.'),
      '02_multiple_testing_results':('Multiple testing','Part 1 results','Numbers of SNP–gene pairs and genes passing nominal, genome-wide BH and Bonferroni thresholds across 6,597,508 tests.','Associations are unadjusted for ancestry or technical covariates; LD induces dependence between tests.'),
      '03_significant_cis_eqtls_by_chromosome':('Chromosomal distribution','Part 1 results','Genome-wide significant cis-eQTL associations summarized by autosome using BH and Bonferroni correction over the full testing family.','Chromosomes differ in gene and tested-variant density; counts are not per-variant discovery rates.'),
      '04_snps_tested_per_gene':('Cis-window coverage','Part 1 supplementary','Distribution of tested cis-SNP counts per eligible protein-coding gene using gene bodies plus/minus 500 kb.','Panel coverage and gene length affect the number of tests.'),
      '05_manhattan_strongest_cis_eqtl_per_gene':('Strongest gene associations','Part 1 results','Strongest cis-eQTL association per tested protein-coding gene, arranged across the 22 autosomes.','One point per gene; this is not a genome-wide single-phenotype GWAS Manhattan plot.'),
      '06_RPS26_association_and_expression_by_genotype':('RPS26 association and expression','Part 1 results','Regional cis-eQTL evidence for RPS26 and expression stratified by its lead-variant genotype among matched public individuals.','Heavy-tailed expression and the no-covariate model limit interpretation; association is not causality.'),
      '07_RPS26_locuszoom':('RPS26 regional associations','Part 1 results','Preserved RPS26 regional association visualization with LD and gene-position annotations on GRCh37.','Matplotlib-generated regional figure, not actual LocusZoom software output; LD does not identify a causal SNP.'),
    })
    for folder in ['results/part1/figures','results/part2/figures','results/part2/pca','results/week7/figures',
                   'results/expression_prediction/pilot150_v2/figures','results/part2/exploration/figures']:
        for p in sorted((root/folder).glob('*')):
            if p.suffix not in ('.png','.pdf','.svg'):continue
            stem=p.stem
            if stem in captions:purpose,section,caption,limit=captions[stem]
            else:
                purpose=stem.split('_',1)[-1].replace('_',' ');section='Part 1 supplementary' if stem.startswith('04_') else 'Part 1 results'
                caption='Preserved Part 1 figure: '+purpose+'. See the existing Part 1 methods/results notes for statistical definitions.'
                limit='Original image preserved without regeneration; original resolution and original no-covariate eQTL model apply.'
            source='Saved Part 1 associations and GENCODE v12' if '/part1/' in str(p) else 'Saved Part 1 Table A, GENCODE v12 and lifted ENCFF421XIL' if '/week7/' in str(p) else 'Saved public EUR PRSs, selected weights and LD-pruned reference PCA'
            if '/expression_prediction/' in str(p):source='Frozen training-only 150-gene pilot, held-out predictions and verified Week 7 annotations'
            if '/part2/exploration/' in str(p):source='Saved harmonized disease GWASs, public EUR reference scores and original genome-wide eQTL Table A'
            inventory.append(dict(Filename=str(p.relative_to(root)),Purpose=purpose,Data_Source=source,Paper_Section=section,Caption=caption,Limitations=limit))
    pd.DataFrame(inventory).to_csv(root/'results/figure_inventory.tsv',sep='\t',index=False)
    from scripts.export_paper_figures import export_paper_figures
    collection=export_paper_figures()
    print('Wrote public methods/results notes, figure inventory and LaTeX examples.')
    print(f"Collected {collection['Distinct_Figures']} distinct figures in {collection['Folder']}")
if __name__=='__main__':main()
